WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.470
How did Zeus come to rule a whole universe
of various divine pantheons?

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:08.930
How advanced has human technology become after
all these crises and infinity wars?

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:10.850
And where was Sif for the last 10 years?

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.810
These are the biggest unanswered questions
in "Thor: Love and Thunder."

00:00:14.445 --> 00:00:17.720
Thor's iconic weapon Mjolnir has always been
something of a mystery.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:18.930
Was it ever truly sentient?

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:22.179
How did it work, beyond the cop-out answer
of "magic?"

00:00:22.179 --> 00:00:31.119
"Whosoever holds this hammer, if he be worthy,
shall possess the power of Thor."

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:36.040
How could it actually gauge a wielder's worthiness,
and does it psychically sense Thor's intentions

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.040
when thrown?

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.790
Similarly, everyone's favorite monosyllabic
tree person, Groot, has always been a bit

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:41.790
of an enigma.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.880
How can he regrow from a single twig?

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:47.450
Does that mean that his consciousness is stored
in every little sliver of his wood?

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.390
If you cut him in half, do you end up with
two Groots?

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:52.310
What even is Groot?

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:55.560
"I am Groot!"

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.550
Right, well, that one might be answered already.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:03.600
Regardless, the reason we bring up these two
mysteries together is that, as of "Infinity

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.659
War," the two sets of questions now all apply
to one item: Stormbreaker, the magic ax made

00:01:08.659 --> 00:01:10.159
from the arm of Groot.

00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:14.340
Stormbreaker has always been a bit of an unknown
in the MCU, given that it seems to have all

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.310
the powers of Mjolnir without the benefit
of any of Odin's spells.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:22.700
And "Thor: Love and Thunder" ramps up the
weapon's power and mysterious nature tenfold.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:26.640
In the movie, the weapon demonstrates obvious
sentience, and even a personality, by acting

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.869
petulant toward Thor when the God of Thunder
attempts to summon his old pal Mjolnir.

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:35.329
There may be an answer, one subtly revealed
early in the film: When Thor plants Stormbreaker

00:01:35.329 --> 00:01:37.229
in the dirt, it grows roots.

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:41.259
Just like Groot was reborn from a tiny, burnt
little twig, it seems Stormbreaker may be

00:01:41.259 --> 00:01:43.670
attempting to regrow itself from its handle
as well.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.719
In the Marvel comics, the origin of All-Black
the Necrosword, the deadly weapon wielded

00:01:48.719 --> 00:01:51.490
by Gorr the God Butcher, is thoroughly explained.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:55.369
Over the course of multiple comic series,
the long history of the Necrosword, its true

00:01:55.369 --> 00:02:00.210
nature as a living Symbiote, and its creator
— the dark god Knull — have been fully

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:01.210
fleshed out.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:05.840
But throughout the weapon's MCU debut in "Thor:
Love and Thunder," the weapon is never explained

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:06.840
satisfactorily.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.349
It is evident from the fact that every god
seems aware of and afraid of the Necrosword

00:02:10.349 --> 00:02:14.750
that the weapon's history is long and storied,
but no portion of that story is ever revealed.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:19.349
When we first see the sword, it lays motionless
next to the body of its former wielder, an

00:02:19.349 --> 00:02:20.560
unnamed, dead god.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.040
Beyond that, we never learn much about the
sword, aside from its habit of slowly poisoning

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.920
and maddening its wielder.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.680
As of a post-credits scene in "Spider-Man:
No Way Home," at least, the Venom symbiote

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:35.120
does indeed exist in the MCU proper, so it's
possible that a more explicit connection to

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.149
symbiotes will be revealed in the future.

00:02:37.766 --> 00:02:42.409
Okay, yes, the real-world answer to the question
of Sif's whereabouts since the events of "Thor:

00:02:42.409 --> 00:02:46.990
The Dark World" has an easy enough answer:
The actor that portrays Sif, Jaime Alexander,

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:50.730
was busy filming her hit NBC crime-drama series
"Blindspot."

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:54.590
But every Hollywood production is limited
by availability, budget, and an endless amount

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:58.549
of other constraints, and those films, especially
if they're in a connected universe like the

00:02:58.549 --> 00:03:01.900
MCU, need an in-canon explanation as well.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:06.709
Lady Sif was absent from the big screen for
nine years, and according to "Thor: Love and

00:03:06.709 --> 00:03:09.340
Thunder," the Asgardian warrior was... out
there.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.340
Somewhere.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.340
Fighting something.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:12.680
For some reason.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:16.910
When we catch up with Sif via an interstellar
distress signal in "Thor: Love and Thunder,"

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:21.680
she's in the midst of combat, fighting alongside
some unnamed alien gods against Gorr, and

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:22.750
losing badly.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:26.650
However, that small bit of information is
where the statements end and the questions

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:27.650
begin.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:28.750
Where was Sif all this time?

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.939
Was she not on Asgard when it fell?

00:03:30.939 --> 00:03:32.390
If not, why?

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:35.719
Why didn't she return to New Asgard to help
her people rebuild their civilization?

00:03:35.719 --> 00:03:39.769
Who were the gods she fought with, and how
did she know about Gorr well in advance of

00:03:39.769 --> 00:03:41.090
Thor and the Guardians of the Galaxy?

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:45.859
In the MCU, for some reason, it seems that
Earth's gods are much more important to the

00:03:45.859 --> 00:03:49.299
cosmos and its many goings-on than any other
planet's.

00:03:49.299 --> 00:03:53.860
Just like Sif's absence, there is a simple
real-world explanation for this Earth bias:

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:56.370
The people who make and watch the movies are
all from Earth.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.299
And while that certainly explains why we watch
movies about Thor instead of movies about

00:04:00.299 --> 00:04:04.829
some random god from another random rock in
space, "Thor: Love and Thunder" added a couple

00:04:04.829 --> 00:04:09.200
of remarkable details to the MCU that make
Earth's central status a bit more baffling.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.530
Firstly, Omnipotence City, the meeting place
of all the universe's, or at least galaxy's,

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:19.519
many pantheons, is ruled over by Zeus, of
the Earth-based Greek pantheon.

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:23.889
Given that NASA estimates our galaxy to contain
over 100 billion planets, the odds that a

00:04:23.889 --> 00:04:28.660
god from Earth would be in charge are, well,
about one in 100 billion.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:33.370
Secondly, the film's second half centers around
Gorr's attempts to reach the abstract cosmic

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:38.120
entity known as Eternity, and for some reason,
the Bifrost is the one key to getting there.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:42.199
For some reason, Earth's Norse Pantheon — and
specifically its God of Thunder — contains

00:04:42.199 --> 00:04:44.810
the sole means of access to Eternity.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:49.270
Surely one of the other 100 billion planets
has a more suitable pantheon and more suitable

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:50.440
god for getting to Eternity.

00:04:51.781 --> 00:04:55.220
Earth's gods aren't the only beings whose
status in the cosmic order is in question

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:59.470
as of "Thor: Love and Thunder" — its regular
humans' status is, too.

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:03.470
When daily life in New Asgard is finally revealed
in greater detail, we see that it employs

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:08.010
its own police force, which is equipped with
armor and weapons that look purposefully half-human

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:09.590
and half-Asgardian.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:14.240
That suggests an intermingling of technology,
just as their flying tour boats do.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.260
Given the fact that at least some humans now
have access to Asgardian technology in this

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:22.000
story, and since the entire plot of "Black
Panther" revolves around the hyper-advanced

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.750
country finally sharing its technology with
the rest of the world as well, we can only

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:29.910
assume that the humans of the MCU are now
centuries more advanced than we are.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:35.100
And there's another major asset that the MCU
humans have that we don't: Dr. Jane Foster.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:38.949
When explaining her book and the research
behind it, Foster reveals that she has essentially

00:05:38.949 --> 00:05:43.380
solved the physics behind Einstein-Rosen Bridges,
aka wormholes.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:47.319
Given that many physicists believe that wormholes
are based on laws from both General Relativity

00:05:47.319 --> 00:05:52.010
and Quantum Mechanics, Foster's findings heavily
imply that she has at least partially cracked

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:55.770
the Theory of Everything/Grand Unifying Theory.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:06:00.330
Even being conservative, Foster's theoretical
advances, paired with newly-accessible Wakandan

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:05.020
and Asgardian tech, means that the humans
of Earth 616 have a bright future close at

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:05.582
hand.

00:06:06.381 --> 00:06:11.240
In both the Marvel comics and the MCU, the
awesome, nigh-infinite power of Odin is named

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:12.240
the Odinforce.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.490
It is one of the strongest and most versatile
energy sources in the universe, making Odin

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:21.349
more fearsome and formidable than nearly any
other god, and even as much as some cosmic

00:06:21.349 --> 00:06:22.860
entities like Galactus.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:27.100
The Odinforce is what allows Odin to empower
his spear Gungnir, the Destroyer, and even

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:31.060
Thor's trusty Mjolnir, so it is clearly a
force to be reckoned with.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:35.560
That's why its absence from the MCU after
Odin's death is not only noticeable but raises

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:38.419
the question: Wait, where did it go?

00:06:38.419 --> 00:06:42.889
In the comics, the Odinforce is passed down
from king to king — Bor wielded the Borforce

00:06:42.889 --> 00:06:47.091
and then passed it to Odin as the Odinforce,
and he, in turn, passed it to Thor as the

00:06:47.091 --> 00:06:48.091
Thorforce.

00:06:48.091 --> 00:06:53.560
But the MCU never explains nor shows this,
and that leads to two all-important questions.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.750
First, did Thor ever inherit that tremendous
power?

00:06:56.750 --> 00:07:00.330
And if not, might instead the power have passed
to Asgard's king, Valkyrie?

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:04.569
In the past, the line of kings has always
been genetic as well as political, but ever

00:07:04.569 --> 00:07:08.970
since the end of "Avengers: Endgame," when
Thor made Valkyrie king, the two lines are

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:12.599
separate, and so the MCU may very well have
its own Thorforce, Valkyrieforce, both, or

00:07:12.599 --> 00:07:13.599
neither.

00:07:14.951 --> 00:07:19.289
If there is one question that the Marvel movies
of Phase 4 have repeatedly raised, it is:

00:07:19.289 --> 00:07:22.610
To what extent are humanity, power, and destiny
all related?

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:26.960
In "Spider-Man: No Way Home," we saw a human,
Dr. Strange, tear apart the fabric of the

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:27.960
multiverse.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.889
In "Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness,"
we saw both Strange and another human, the

00:07:31.889 --> 00:07:34.889
Scarlet Witch, do the same on an even larger
scale.

00:07:34.889 --> 00:07:38.860
Then, in "Thor: Love and Thunder," we see
another human, Jane Foster, wield the weapon

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:43.530
of a god, become a god herself, and fight
toe-to-toe with gods — as did her antagonist,

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:44.530
Gorr.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:48.979
And with the increasing number of uber-powerful
beings populating the MCU that are not gods,

00:07:48.979 --> 00:07:54.220
like the Watcher and the Celestials, the question
of what exactly a god is has become more vexing

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:55.220
than ever.

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:59.860
A particularly puzzling detail about the MCU's
divine beings is that their continued existence

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:04.050
and power don't seem to be connected to the
one thing you would expect to be guaranteed:

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:05.050
worship.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:09.039
When a desperate, broken Gorr comes across
his own god and explains that all of his worshippers

00:08:09.039 --> 00:08:12.990
are dead, the god admits that he is happy
to see them go — he doesn't need them, nor

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:15.139
does he care about their existence.

00:08:15.139 --> 00:08:16.939
"All gods will die"

00:08:16.939 --> 00:08:21.360
The gods of the MCU are clearly not determined
by worship or by power — otherwise, the

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:24.130
reality-rewriting Scarlet Witch would be one
of their greatest.

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:28.889
So far, we've covered the fact that MCU's
humans are stronger than ever, both in the

00:08:28.889 --> 00:08:32.930
growing number of super-powered beings in
their ranks and in the hyper-advanced technology

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:35.310
they either have or will soon have.

00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:39.240
We've also covered the fact that the MCU's
gods seem to merely be a loose assortment

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:44.070
of powerful beings that aren't powerful enough
to be considered abstract entities like Eternity

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:45.230
or the Watcher.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:50.130
The obvious question, then, is what exactly
does the cosmic hierarchy of the MCU look

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:51.130
like?

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:55.800
Two Celestials attended Zeus's meeting of
the gods, even though they aren't gods themselves.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:01.190
Every god and their uncle seems aware of Eternity,
and so presumably, also its twin, Infinity.

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:05.550
Do universal constants and abstract beings
cohabitate freely with gods?

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:09.950
We know gods do with people, so that implies
a surprisingly open and tolerant universal

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:10.950
community.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:15.100
Do the other universal constants that we haven't
met, like Galactus and the Phoenix, act in

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.040
a similarly casual manner?

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.590
And we've met two Elders of the Universe so
far — the Collector and the Grandmaster

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:24.490
— so are they gods, abstract beings, or
something else?

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:28.440
It's surprising that a universe so jam-packed
with near-omnipotent super-beings of varying

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.370
backgrounds hasn't destroyed itself yet.

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:35.010
One of the biggest reveals in "Thor: Love
and Thunder" is that Valhalla, the fabled

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:38.360
resting place for deceased Asgardian gods,
is real.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:42.870
Upon her death, Foster is welcomed there by
Heimdall, who looks happy, healthy, and serene.

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:45.910
So if Heimdall is in Valhalla, is Odin there,
too?

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:48.510
What about other fallen Asgardian warriors?

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:51.769
And if Foster is welcome there because she
proved herself worthy and wielded Mjolnir

00:09:51.769 --> 00:09:56.290
in defense of Asgard, then surely everyone's
favorite Mjolnir wielder, Captain America,

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:57.690
could end up there as well?

00:09:57.690 --> 00:10:01.580
Perhaps more important than any of these questions
is the matter of Valhalla's accessibility.

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:06.380
For one thing, the very job definition of
a Valkyrie is to freely travel between Valhalla

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:10.540
and the other realms, so can King Valkyrie
just pop in to visit Foster and Heimdall?

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:12.970
Can she relay messages between them and the
living?

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:14.240
Can she bring them back?

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:19.560
Alongside Valhalla, the MCU has already confirmed
the existence of two other afterlives — the

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:23.839
Ancestral Plane of the Wakandan people and
the Duat of the Egyptian people.

00:10:23.839 --> 00:10:27.779
Unless Marvel plans to make the exceedingly
bold claim that those three religions are

00:10:27.779 --> 00:10:32.060
the only three that got it right, then in
general, we now know that afterlives exist

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:34.589
— and seemingly, all of them.

00:10:34.589 --> 00:10:40.890
"The dead will drag you down into the Duat,
where you will remain forever, frozen in sand."

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:45.339
As we mentioned, Steve Rogers wielded Mjolnir,
so will he be given a choice between Valhalla

00:10:45.339 --> 00:10:46.690
and a Christian Heaven?

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:50.970
Tony Stark saved half the sentient life in
the galaxy but died, apparently, as a non-believer.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:55.370
Will he spend an eternity of punishment in
some version of Hell?

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:59.390
As tricky as it would be to stick the landing
on this existential conundrum, an answer from

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:04.310
Marvel would be a major relief when it comes
to the fate of some fan-favorite characters.

